# The dotted crotchet and its quaver: a dot adds half, the note that finishes a bar, a dotted note
# in quavers, a written bar's beats, long-short tapped, and two bars of the child's own.
lesson music-dotted-rhythms v=1 format=teach grade=4 unit=4 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "The dotted crotchet and its quaver"
  goal "Read, count and tap dotted rhythms, where a dot adds half a note's value again."

  look {
    scene 21x14 {
      notes bar clef=treble notes=[G4, A4, B4, C5] values=[1.5, 0.5, 1, 1] meter=4 letters=false at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      A dot after a note makes it longer by half of itself. A minim is 2 beats, so a dotted minim is
      3. A crotchet is 1 beat, so a dotted crotchet is one and a half. That leaves half a beat
      before the next beat, so a dotted crotchet is often followed by a quaver: long, short. Count
      it as 1, 2 and, 3, 4: the quaver comes on the and just before beat 3. Dotted rhythms give a
      tune a skip, like a horse cantering.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.dot-value k=0 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show music.dot-fill v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=1
    level easy {
      worked music.dot-count v=0 level=medium
    }
    show music.dot-tap
    show music.dot-quavers k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=1
    show music.dot-value k=1 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show music.dot-tap-two
    level medium hard {
      show music.dot-count v=1 hard-v=2
      show music.dot-fill v=2
    }
    level easy {
      show music.dot-quavers k=1
      show music.dot-fill v=1
    }
    level hard {
      show music.dot-quavers k=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.dot-why r=0 easy-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.dot-write v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A dot adds half the note again: a dotted crotchet is one and a half beats, and its quaver fills the half beat after it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a dot adds half of a note's own value, so a dotted crotchet lasts one and a
    half beats and leaves half a beat for the quaver that usually follows it, giving the long-short
    rhythm. The wrong ideas to watch for are doubling a note for its dot, and tapping the quaver on
    the beat instead of just before the next one. In the working, look for the child writing each
    note's beats under it, and counting "1, 2 and, 3, 4" while tapping. The core works out what a
    dot adds, finishes a bar, turns a dotted note into quavers, adds a bar of dotted notes and taps
    two dotted bars, checked against the beat; the two-star asks why a quaver follows the dot, and
    the three-star is two bars written and clapped, which you look at and listen to with the notice
    list.

    If the dot is stuck, draw a crotchet as a strip of two squares (two quavers) and the dot as one
    more square. Off the page, say "hump-ty dump-ty" and notice the long-short in each word. This
    lesson meets Russia's federal work programme in music for primary school, which names the dotted
    note and the dotted rhythm, 「Нота с точкой… Пунктирный ритм」 (as quoted in the audit of grades 5
    and 6), and Japan's 2017 course of study for music, 第3 2(9), the notes and rests to understand
    and use as they work in music.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the dots are on a minim and a crotchet, the bars to finish need a quaver and a crotchet, counting a bar's beats is worked once in the teacher's pen, the dotted notes turned into quavers are a crotchet and a minim, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the two bars each have one dotted pair."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the dots are on a semibreve and a quaver, the bars to finish need a crotchet and a dotted crotchet, the dotted notes turned into quavers and semiquavers are a minim and a quaver, the bar's beats include a dotted minim, and the two bars are in three-time with a rest."
  }
}
